  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 253 AM EDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nConditions will remain wet and unsettled through the extended period \nas the previously discussed pattern remains in place through the \nweekend. Towards the end of the weekend, the aforementioned upper \nlevel trough will eject eastward, dragging a surface front across \nthe southeastern US. At the same time, weak TUTTs will continue to \napproach the peninsula.  Easterly to southeasterly flow will \ncontinue across our region, with PWATs continuing to trend near \nnormal or slightly above normal (1.9-2.1 inch range). Each afternoon \ncould bring scattered to numerous showers and storms, starting along \nthe East coast sea breeze boundary then proceeding inland into the \nlate afternoon hours. Similarly to the short term period, gusty \nwinds, heavy rainfall and frequent lightning will all remain \npossible hazards.\n\nStarting next week, once the trough and front progress eastward, \nmodels suggest the two flanking ridges over the US and western \nAtlantic will start to merge, extending across the entire region. \nAfternoon showers and thunderstorms will remain likely each \nafternoon, with coverage maximized across the interior and southwest \nFL thanks to the continuing easterly flow. \n\nTemperatures through the period will remain seasonably hot, with \nhighs in the low to mid 90s each afternoon and overnight lows in the \nlow 80s across the coastlines, and mid 70s over the interior.",
